  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-770",
              "name": "Anonymous room creation resource exhaustion via Socket.IO reconnect bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Anonymous room creation resource exhaustion via Socket.IO reconnect bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The anonymous `host:create` and `solo:create` Socket.IO events enforce their create budget only on a single socket, not on the originating source. An attacker can open fresh connections to bypass the nominal `3/minute` limit, create large numbers of rooms, and keep those rooms resident long enough to cause meaningful memory growth and availability impact."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The anonymous `host:create` and `solo:create` Socket.IO events enforce their create budget only on a single socket, not on the originating source. An attacker can open fresh connections to bypass the nominal `3/minute` limit, create large numbers of rooms, and keep those rooms resident long enough to cause meaningful memory growth and availability impact.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can create thousands of concurrent in-memory rooms from one source and retain them for long enough to degrade or exhaust the Node.js process. Local validation showed linear heap growth and persistent room retention after disconnect, and the live target reproduced the same per-socket bypass behavior for both room-creation events.\n\n1. Enforce anonymous room-creation quotas with a process-wide source key derived from the server-observed client address, rather than from `socket.data`, so reconnecting or opening parallel transports does not reset the budget.\n2. Share the quota across both `host:create` and `solo:create`, and bound the backing tracker with expiry and hard caps so the mitigation does not itself become an unbounded map.\n3. Shorten eviction for empty never-started rooms, especially solo and lobby-only rooms, so disconnecting immediately after creation does not reserve memory for fifteen minutes.\n4. Keep the longer reconnect grace only for rooms that have already started or still contain connected participants, and reject or tear down room creation bursts before allocating full room state.",
                "markdown": "The anonymous `host:create` and `solo:create` Socket.IO events enforce their create budget only on a single socket, not on the originating source. An attacker can open fresh connections to bypass the nominal `3/minute` limit, create large numbers of rooms, and keep those rooms resident long enough to cause meaningful memory growth and availability impact.\n\nAn unauthenticated remote attacker can create thousands of concurrent in-memory rooms from one source and retain them for long enough to degrade or exhaust the Node.js process. Local validation showed linear heap growth and persistent room retention after disconnect, and the live target reproduced the same per-socket bypass behavior for both room-creation events.\n\n1. Enforce anonymous room-creation quotas with a process-wide source key derived from the server-observed client address, rather than from `socket.data`, so reconnecting or opening parallel transports does not reset the budget.\n2. Share the quota across both `host:create` and `solo:create`, and bound the backing tracker with expiry and hard caps so the mitigation does not itself become an unbounded map.\n3. Shorten eviction for empty never-started rooms, especially solo and lobby-only rooms, so disconnecting immediately after creation does not reserve memory for fifteen minutes.\n4. Keep the longer reconnect grace only for rooms that have already started or still contain connected participants, and reject or tear down room creation bursts before allocating full room state."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-770",
                  "stride:D"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/770.html"
            }
          ],
          "version": "1.5.3"
        }
      },
