          "partialFingerprints": {
            "primaryLocationLineHash": "ba7511a8a309e060573bf4cb4e695913e80814e7cdb65b378e5a9655eb541cfa"
          },
          "properties": {
            "security-severity": "7.5",
            "strix_vuln_class_hash": "31f0a3984ec52ca2bd6a3e0d2435c619f155f8047cb1a5e3eef29bca637e1715",
            "strix": {
              "id": "vuln-0001",
              "severity": "high",
              "cvss": 7.5,
              "timestamp": "2026-08-23 04:19:31 UTC",
              "target": "http://host.docker.internal:3123",
              "endpoint": "/socket.io",
              "cwe": "CWE-770",
              "impact": "An unauthenticated remote attacker can create thousands of concurrent in-memory rooms from one source and retain them for long enough to degrade or exhaust the Node.js process. Local validation showed linear heap growth and persistent room retention after disconnect, and the live target reproduced the same per-socket bypass behavior for both room-creation events.",
              "technical_analysis": "The Socket.IO guard applies the limit to `socket.data.rateLimits`, so every new transport starts with a fresh counter. The `host:create` and `solo:create` handlers therefore inherit a per-socket limit rather than a source-based limit.\n\n```javascript title=server/sockets.js startLineNumber=20 endLineNumber=30\nfunction takeRateToken(socket, name, limit, windowMs) {\n  const now = Date.now();\n  socket.data.rateLimits ||= new Map();\n  const recent = (socket.data.rateLimits.get(name) || []).filter((at) => now - at < windowMs);\n  if (recent.length >= limit) {\n    socket.data.rateLimits.set(name, recent);\n    return false;\n  }\n  recent.push(now);\n  socket.data.rateLimits.set(name, recent);\n  return true;\n}\n```\n\nThe affected creation handlers both rely on that per-socket guard before allocating a new `Room` object and storing it in the process-wide `rooms` map.\n\n```javascript title=server/sockets.js startLineNumber=123 endLineNumber=145\nsocket.on('host:create', guarded(socket, 'host:create', { limit: 3, ack: true }, async (payload, cb) => {\n  if (typeof cb !== 'function') return;\n  try {\n    let code;\n    do { code = makeRoomCode(); } while (rooms.has(code));\n    const requestedConfig = allowInternalCreateConfig ? (payload?.config || {}) : pickHostEditableConfig(payload?.config);\n    const r = new Room(io, code, requestedConfig, destroyRoom, { allowClientScoredCompetitive });\n    rooms.set(code, r);\n    ...\n```\n\n```javascript title=server/sockets.js startLineNumber=215 endLineNumber=228\nsocket.on('solo:create', guarded(socket, 'solo:create', { limit: 3, ack: true }, ({ name } = {}, cb) => {\n  if (typeof cb !== 'function') return;\n  let code;\n  do { code = makeRoomCode(); } while (rooms.has(code));\n  const r = new Room(io, code, {}, destroyRoom, { allowClientScoredCompetitive });\n  r.solo = true;\n  rooms.set(code, r);\n  ...\n```\n\nRoom destruction is also delayed after the attacker disconnects. When no host or player remains connected, `handleDisconnect()` schedules a fixed `15 * 60 * 1000` empty-room timer. In the lobby, `scheduleLobbyKick()` removes disconnected players after `60000` ms, but that does not destroy the room itself, so empty never-started rooms remain resident.\n\n```javascript title=server/room.js startLineNumber=285 endLineNumber=293\nscheduleLobbyKick(playerId) {\n  if (this.phase !== 'lobby') return;\n  this.setTimer(`kick:${playerId}`, () => {\n    const p = this.players.get(playerId);\n    if (this.phase === 'lobby' && p && !p.connected && !p.socketId) {\n      this.players.delete(playerId);\n      this.broadcastPlayers();\n    }\n  }, 60000);\n}\n```\n\n```javascript title=server/room.js startLineNumber=439 endLineNumber=447\nhandleDisconnect(socket) {\n  if (socket.id === this.hostSocketId) this.hostSocketId = null;\n  const changed = this.disconnectSocketPlayers(socket.id);\n  if (changed) this.broadcastPlayers();\n  const anyConnected =\n    this.hostSocketId || [...this.players.values()].some((p) => p.connected);\n  if (!anyConnected) {\n    this.setTimer('empty', () => this.onEmpty(this), 15 * 60 * 1000);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nValidated behavior matched the code: attempt 4 on the same socket is blocked, while the same client succeeds again by reconnecting and issuing one create per fresh socket. Because each successful call allocates a new room that stays resident long after disconnect, the bypass translates directly into unauthenticated memory and availability pressure.",
              "remediation_steps": "1. Enforce anonymous room-creation quotas with a process-wide source key derived from the server-observed client address, rather than from `socket.data`, so reconnecting or opening parallel transports does not reset the budget.\n2. Share the quota across both `host:create` and `solo:create`, and bound the backing tracker with expiry and hard caps so the mitigation does not itself become an unbounded map.\n3. Shorten eviction for empty never-started rooms, especially solo and lobby-only rooms, so disconnecting immediately after creation does not reserve memory for fifteen minutes.\n4. Keep the longer reconnect grace only for rooms that have already started or still contain connected participants, and reject or tear down room creation bursts before allocating full room state.",
              "poc": {
                "description": "1. Connect a Socket.IO client to `/socket.io` and emit `host:create` four times on the same socket; observe that attempts 1-3 succeed and attempt 4 returns `Too many requests — try again shortly.`.\n2. Open a fresh Socket.IO connection for each request and emit one `host:create` on each new socket; observe that all fresh-socket creates succeed despite the prior rate-limit response.\n3. Repeat the same two checks for `solo:create`; again, attempts 1-3 on one socket succeed, attempt 4 is blocked, and fresh-socket requests continue to succeed.\n4. Against a local instance of the same codebase, automate fresh-socket `solo:create` requests at scale, immediately disconnect each socket, and measure heap usage and `rooms.size` before and after the burst.\n5. Wait 65 seconds and inspect room state; observe that disconnected host and solo rooms are still resident and retain an `empty` timer instead of being destroyed promptly.",
                "script_available": true
              }
            }
          }
        }
      ]
    }
